# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit the real .env.
#
# MONITOR_TARGETS: `;`-separated `Label|rpcUrlOrEnvVar|executorAddress`
#   list -- one entry per network to watch. `|`/`;` specifically because
#   a literal RPC URL already contains `:` and often gets pasted next to
#   other comma-separated config, so those two couldn't be the
#   delimiters. The middle segment is looked up as an env var first (so
#   you can point at SEPOLIA_RPC_URL, BASE_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL,
#   ARBITRUM_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL etc. from the project root .env without
#   copying the URL here), or used as a literal RPC URL if no env var by
#   that name exists. wss:// is recommended over https:// where your
#   provider supports it -- real-time push instead of polling, same as
#   off-chain-bot's RPC_URL.
#
#   Example (reusing root .env's network vars, after `export $(cat ../.env)`
#   or otherwise having them in this process's environment):
#     MONITOR_TARGETS=Ethereum Sepolia|SEPOLIA_RPC_URL|0xYourExecutor;Base Sepolia|BASE_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL|0xYourExecutor
#
#   Example (literal URLs, fully self-contained):
#     MONITOR_TARGETS=Ethereum Sepolia|https://eth-sepolia.g.alchemy.com/v2/KEY|0xYourExecutor
MONITOR_TARGETS=

# Optional: a Slack or Discord incoming-webhook URL. Every event gets
# POSTed there in addition to stdout -- console output alone only helps
# if someone is actually staring at the terminal, which defeats the
# point for anything meant to run unattended. Leave blank to log to
# console only.
MONITOR_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Optional: on startup, scan back this many blocks and alert on anything
# already-emitted that was missed (default: 0 = skip, only watch new
# events from here forward). Set this after any period the monitor
# wasn't running, so a gap in uptime doesn't become a silent gap in
# coverage.
MONITOR_LOOKBACK_BLOCKS=

# Optional: block-range size per eth_getLogs call while catching up
# (default: 10). Several RPC providers cap this well below any useful
# lookback window and reject a wider request outright rather than
# truncating it -- confirmed live against Alchemy's free tier, which
# caps at exactly 10. Raise this if your provider allows more, to cut
# down round trips on a long MONITOR_LOOKBACK_BLOCKS.
MONITOR_LOOKBACK_CHUNK_SIZE=

# Optional Telegram bot -- both required together, or leave both blank.
# Every alert above gets sent here too, plus /status, /pause, /resume
# against shared/data/bot.db's circuit-breaker state (see monitor.js's
# top-of-file comment). Works with MONITOR_TARGETS left empty too, if you
# only want Telegram control over off-chain-bot's circuit breaker without
# watching any executor's on-chain events.
#
# Setup:
#   1. Message @BotFather on Telegram, send /newbot, follow its prompts --
#      it gives you a token that looks like 123456789:AAExampleTokenHere.
#   2. Message your new bot anything (it can't message you first), then
#      visit https://api.telegram.org/bot<token>/getUpdates in a browser
#      -- your chat id is the "id" field under "chat" in the response.
#
# TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID is a real authorization check, not just a routing
# hint -- a bot's username is publicly discoverable, so without this
# check anyone who finds it could pause/resume real trading. Commands
# from any other chat are logged and ignored.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=
